"april99 (https://www.vos.noaa.gov/MWL/apr1999.pdf)\nciteturn23view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 108; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view0\",\"pattern\":\"circular particle\"}); Total lines: 6538\nL940@P18: so on (Figure 2). In fact, these\nL941@P18: water particles actually oscillate in\nL942@P18: two dimensions, moving in\nL943@P18: (almost) perfect vertical circles\nL944@P18: (usually referred to as particle\nL945@P18: orbits). After a complete cycle of\nL946@P18: the wave, each particle comes\nL947@P18: back to (almost) where it started\nL948@P18: one cycle earlier. This is clear\nL949@P18: when a float is on the water\nL950@P18: surface. When a wave goes by, the\nL951@P18: float moves forward as it moves\nL952@P18: upward, and then it moves back\u0002ward as it moves downward. One\nL953@P18: also notes in Figure 2 that the\nL954@P18: wave has an effect on the water\nL955@P18: column below it. The circular\nL956@P18: particle orbits become smaller as\nL957@P18: one goes deeper, disappearing at a\nL958@P18: location of no wave motion at a\nL959@P18: depth equal to approximately half\nL960@P18: the wavelength. (If the water is too\nL961@P18: shallow for a location of no wave\nL962@P18: motion to occur, this shallow\u0002water wave will have elliptical\nL963@P18: particle orbits, as well as other\nL964@P18: different characteristics, which we\nL965@P18: will look at in a later Physical\nL966@P18: Oceanography column.)\nL967@P18: A key point to remember is that it\nL968@P18: is the shape of the water surface\nL969@P18: (and the energy) that is propagat\u0002ing away, not the water particles\nL970@P18: themselves. [“(almost)” was used\nL971@P18: twice in the previous paragraph\nL972@P18: because, to be very precise, there\nL973@P18: is a very, very small transport of\nL974@P18: water forward with each wave\nL975@P18: cycle, but this is insignificantly\nL976@P18: small compared with the speed of\nL977@P18: the propagating shape of the\nL978@P18: surface.]\nL979@P18: At this point we should define\nL980@P18: some terms related to waves\nL981@P18: (some of which you probably have\nL982@P18: already seen before), which are\nL983@P18: illustrated in Figure 3. Note,\nL984@P18: however, that the wave shown in\nL985@P18: Figure 3 is an idealized wave (or\nL986@P18: one component making up a\nL987@P18: wave). The highest point that the\nL988@P18: water surface reaches is the crest\nL989@P18: of the wave; the lowest point is\nL990@P18: the trough. The difference from\nL991@P18: trough to crest is the wave height.\nL992@P18: The distance from one crest to the\nL993@P18: next crest, or from one trough to\nL994@P18: the next trough, is the wavelength.\nL995@P18: How Wind Generates Waves\nL996@P18: Continued from page 18\nL997@P18: Continued on Page 20\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFLIP Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/flip)\nciteturn23view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view2\",\"pattern\":\"ˈflip\"}); Total lines: 402\nL15:     * cite2†Games L16:     * cite3†Word of the Day L17:     * cite4†Grammar L18:     * cite8†Wordplay L19:     * cite6†Slang L20:     * cite9†Rhymes L21:     * cite5†Word Finder L22:     * cite7†Newsletters L23:     * cite10†Thesaurus L24:     * cite11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com L25:     * Shop\nL26:     * cite12†Books†shop.merriam-webster.com L34: ## cite15†verb L35: \nL36: cite16†ˈflip  L37: \nL38: flipped; flipping; flips\nL39: \nL40: cite17†Synonyms of flip L41: \nL42: Simple Definition A Simple Definition is available from our Learner's Dictionary to help you understand the meaning faster.\nL43: \nL44: cite18†transitive verb L45: \nL46: 1\nL47: \nL48: : to toss so as to cause to turn over in the air\nL49: \nL50: flip a coin\nL51: \nL52: flip a pancake\nL53: \nL54: also : cite19†toss L55: \nL56: flip me the ball\nL57: \nL58: flip one end of the scarf over your shoulder\nL59: \nL60: 2\nL61: \nL62: a\nL63: \nL64: : to cause to turn and especially to turn over\nL65: \nL66: flipped the car\nL67: \nL68: flipping the pages of a book\nL69: \nL70: b\nL71: : to move with a small quick motion\nL72: \nL73: flip a switch\nL74: \nL75: c\nL76: \nL77: : to turn (something) on or off with a switch\nL78: \nL79: flip on/off the lights\nL80: \nL81: d\nL82: \nL83: : to change or move through (channels, stations, etc.) quickly\nL84: \nL85: flipping channels with the remote control\nL86: \nL87: 3\nL88: \nL89: : to buy and usually renovate (real estate) so as to quickly resell at a higher price\nL90: \nL91: He's flipped three houses for a substantial profit.\nL92: \nL93: 4\nL94: \nL95: : to cause or persuade (a witness) to cooperate in prosecuting a criminal case against an associate\nL96: … wrote that the … probe seemed to have reached the stage where investigators are in the process of flipping witnesses.—Nancy LeTourneau\nL97: \nL98: cite20†intransitive verb L99: \nL100: 1\nL101: \nL102: : to make a twitching or flicking movement\nL103: \nL104: The fish flipped and flopped on the deck.\nL105: \nL106: 2\nL107: \nL108: a\nL109: \nL110: : to turn or roll from one side to the other : cite21†turn over L111: \nL112: The crab had flipped onto its back.\nL113: \nL114: The car flipped over.\nL115: \nL116: b\nL117: \nL118: : to do a cite22†somersault in the air : to do a flip\nL119: \nL120: He flipped off the diving board.\nL121: \nL122: 3\nL123: \nL124: a\nL228: ## Word History\nL229: \nL230: Etymology\nL231: \nL232: Verb, Noun, and Adjective\nL233: \nL234: probably imitative\nL235: \nL236: First Known Use\nL237: \nL238: Verb\nL239: \nL240: 1616, in the meaning defined at cite39†transitive sense 1 L241: \nL242: Noun\nL243: \nL244: 1611, in the meaning defined at cite40†sense 2 L245: \nL246: Adjective\nL247: \n